# Coding principles

Follow `docs/engineering/coding-principles.md` on every change.

- **DRY** — one owner for each rule, shape, or decision; callers reuse it.
- **Orthogonal, modular code** — small modules, one job, narrow surface, loose coupling.
- **Layers of abstraction** — each layer uses only the layer below; never reach past a neighbour into deeper internals.
- **Change blast radius** — prefer designs where a requirement shift hits one module, or a few at the same layer.
- **Do not rely on what you cannot control** — no undocumented library behaviour, platform quirks, or foreign internals; depend on contracts we own or vendors formally guarantee.
- **Shy code** — no unnecessary revelation; talk only through public interfaces.
- **Testability as a coupling check** — if a unit test must import most of the system, fix the boundary.
- **Third-party vendors behind our abstractions** — hide SDKs and persistence products behind our own seams.
- **Flexibility where it matters** — keep flexibility at architecture, deployment, and vendor seams; do not scatter those details through business modules.

When refactoring to these principles, preserve behaviour. Prefer structure and boundary changes over functional changes.
